{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/980703-the-fuil-beannaithe-what-s-up-with-them","id":980703,"title":"The Fuil Beannaithe - what's up with them?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Vampire: The Requiem"],"posts":[{"date":"2016-10-03T04:34:03-07:00","id":980703,"title":"The Fuil Beannaithe - what's up with them?","content":"so I was re-reading Dark Eras, and noticed those guys again. At first I've thought they were simply named dropped as some sort of plot hook or something, for they weren't mentioned anymore in the book. However, after searching for their translated name (Blood Blessed), I've found that they showed up pretty often, treating them like an whole independent Covenant. More than that, I don't seem to get a good picture of who they are and what they supposed to be from that scattered information, and it kinda annoys me. Here is what I've gathered from the book-<br /> <br /> - the first time they are mentioned, they do so as potential threats to the Weihan Cynn together with an unnamed Spanish bloodline which is connected to the Vatican yet to to the Lancea. They are a &quot;rival cult who recently crossed the water&quot;, and it is safe to assume that they are Irish group in origin. The words &quot;rival cult&quot; makes you think about someone who fights over the same resources and niche, so it gives you a pagan association- yet they are mentioned together with the Catholic bloodline, which may mean they are monotheistic.<br /> <br /> -the first time they are mentioned afterwards is in the future of the Era, after the first contact with the &quot;New World&quot; by the British Empire. The Blood Blessed seem to have went to there together with the Weihan Cynn and wanted to protected the native religions from the Sanctified and the Invictus, while Ieldra avoids any official stance. That supports the pagan hypothesis, and shows that they also don't get along with the Lancea.<br /> <br /> -when Tarquin St. John rise to power as the Archbishop of London, he attacks the Blood Blessed as primary targets. Another support to a pagan connection of some kind, or at least another group hated by the Sanctified.<br /> <br /> -the Declaration of Indulgence allows Catholics some air, and both the Sanctified and the Blood Blessed are happy for that. So the Blood Blessed are Catholics, or have some Catholic connection? It also seem that not only the Weihan Cynn see them as rivals, but also the Sanctified (but not as heretics or enemies), for they compete with them over the &quot;dead souls of fledgling Kindred&quot;. Also, it seems that either their cult or one of their cults revolves around Virgin Mary. So they are Catholics who care about native religions and wish to protect them? That sounds weird.<br /> <br /> -the Invictus had enough of the Archbishop, and the Faithless calls, more or less, to an open rebellion against him. St John calls them in order to destroy the rebellion and execute them all, but he didn't evaluated the situation correctly. The Gallows Post as whole, few more Unconquered and about Half of the Blood Blessed stake him and end his reign, putting another prince in his place. While it doesn't show much about the nature of the group (other than that St John hated them, and the feeling was mutual), it does mean that they were important enough and had enough numbers to be a power to consider in the coup. Plus, only half of them joined the attack, unlike the Gallows Post, meaning that while they fed up with the Archbishop, enough of them were either afraid of the consequences or simply didn't cared enough, so they weren't under some existential threat by him.<br /> <br /> and that's it. They are not mentioned any later in the history, or in the London Tonight section. So- does anyone has any ideas about what are they and what they believe in?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/browse.php?author=Shachar%20Gold\" target=\"_blank\">Check my STV content</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/28219-my-weekly-wod-project-program\" target=\"_blank\">Or My Homebrew</a><br /> <br /> &quot;And all our knowledge is, Ourselves to know&quot;- An Essay on Man<br /> <br /> I now blog in <a href=\"https://chroniclesoflostlight.wordpress.com/\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/503-lostlight","name":"LostLight","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar503_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4531"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-05T12:53:08-07:00","id":982278,"title":"","content":"so.. no one has anything to contribute?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/browse.php?author=Shachar%20Gold\" target=\"_blank\">Check my STV content</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/28219-my-weekly-wod-project-program\" target=\"_blank\">Or My Homebrew</a><br /> <br /> &quot;And all our knowledge is, Ourselves to know&quot;- An Essay on Man<br /> <br /> I now blog in <a href=\"https://chroniclesoflostlight.wordpress.com/\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/503-lostlight","name":"LostLight","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar503_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4531"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-05T13:10:34-07:00","id":982293,"title":"","content":"Well, I have some guesses.<br /> <br /> Primarily that it's a proto-Circle of the Crone group with trappings of Catholicism, serving more Mary than anyone else. I'd like to imagine they were sort of a less crass version of the Brothers Grossbart, but either way it's the type of thing that might be, upon first glance, accepted by the Lancea et Sanctum until their true beliefs were made clear. Some aspects of the Danse Macabre 'gonzo covenants' seem to have merged into the second edition covenants, so it's possible this time around the Mother's Army is more directly in contact with the Mother, but I think any real answers that way would have to wait for Secrets of the Covenants.<br /> <br /> Otherwise, it might tie into some historical aspect of the area at the time, which I'm ignorant of. I know a lot of research was done for the era so it's possible there's some real life connections. In this case it seems more likely to just be a faction of the Lancea et Sanctum they might not approve of, and who apparently recognize the tendencies of the covenant when it comes to alternative ideologies.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":886,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","user":"nofather","date":". 10-05-2016, 04:17 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-05T13:45:54-07:00","id":982320,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>LostLight</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/980703-the-fuil-beannaithe-what-s-up-with-them?p=982278#post982278\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">so.. no one has anything to contribute?</div> </div> </div> </div>Well ok, I've been turning over every leaf I can find to discern what VtR has to say for British history. May as well give back:<br /> <br /> I can't point to anything else &quot;official&quot; in VtR. However, in the late 4th century there was a heresy that developed in Britain from one <a href=\"https://www.google.com/url?sa=t&amp;rct=j&amp;q=&amp;esrc=s&amp;source=web&amp;cd=1&amp;cad=rja&amp;uact=8&amp;ved=0ahUKEwiMrtqmv8TPAhWFQiYKHR2YBEUQFggcMAA&amp;url=https%3A%2F%2Fen.wikipedia.org%2Fwiki%2FPelagianism&amp;usg=AFQjCNH75jlcM02LuvNFSA8EG9AxTtdmSA&amp;sig2=Wl6vtteaZdM-d3_phxeEQw&amp;bvm=bv.134495766,d.eWE\" target=\"_blank\">Pelagius</a> that revolved around the rejection of the doctrine of original sin. (From the wikipedia entry: &quot;Pelagius taught that the human will, as created with its abilities by God, was sufficient to live a sinless life, although he believed that God's grace assisted every good work.&quot;)<br /> <br /> Saint Germanus, in the early 5th century, went over in several trips to stamp out Pelagianism, which was both popular among the British aristocracy and followed along with the exile of several groups of elite to Ireland (probably the Ulster area) in 429. In fact, you can read between the lines of his trip to discern just how serious the Catholic Church found the situation to be, as St. Germanus traveled through lands threatened by the Franks, in winter, across the channel, to bring the British to heel on this point.<br /> <br /> Furthermore, you can read a bit into the Church's decision to finally establish a bishopric in Ireland, in 431ish, and conclude Germanus and crew STILL considered the matter serious enough to warrant attention and resources. Note that Christians in Ireland were already present: bishops were not sent without being requested first. This, by the way, is where Patrick enters the story, but we needn't dwell on him now.<br /> <br /> So... what?<br /> <br /> The Blood Blessed might well be a covenant of kindred based around Pelagian thought and developed out of the pagan/christian friction of the Isles in the 5th-6th century, and therefore both divorced from any central doctrinal control by the Sanctified and rather at odds with them. Pelagianism pretty quickly points toward a &quot;live and let live&quot; attitude that would mesh well with the soup of religious practices in the Isles at the time, the constant jockeying between kinglets, and give enough room for individual kindred within the Covenant to push both the pagan tolerance and support angle as well as harder-line &quot;Christian or bust&quot; approaches to purely pagan covenants such as the Weihann Cynn (that name is Saxon, by the way, making its incarnation as such at least contemporary with the Blood Blessed).<br /> <br /> All this might draw together the contradictions for you. It could also be that the Blood Blessed were INTENDED to represent anti-Catholic sentiment in Britain in the same vein as led to the establishment of the Church of England. That doesn't explain their occasional favoring of pagans however, unless there's an element of &quot;our gals better than foreign stiffs&quot; at work.<br /> <br /> Pick your poison.<br /> <br /> --Khanwulf","signature":"","edited":{"userid":12493,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12493-khanwulf","user":"Khanwulf","date":". 10-05-2016, 04:48 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12493-khanwulf","name":"Khanwulf","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12493_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"898"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-06T13:16:32-07:00","id":982882,"title":"","content":"Don't have anything to add, as there is too little about them. But it's very interesting and it escaped me. Perhaps we might see something in the Black Death Dark Era?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/196-max-deltree","name":"Max Deltree","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar196_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"178"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-06T13:31:00-07:00","id":982893,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>nofather</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/980703-the-fuil-beannaithe-what-s-up-with-them?p=982293#post982293\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> In this case it seems more likely to just be a faction of the Lancea et Sanctum they might not approve of, and who apparently recognize the tendencies of the covenant when it comes to alternative ideologies.</div> </div> </div> </div>I tend to assume (from preference) that Longinianism didn't catch on in all the same places as Christianity. Consider how around the time Invictus takes over London and the Westminister Creed was written, Edinburgh is still almost entirely Weihan Cynn (&quot;under other names&quot;). I would guess that the Spears were in the spiritual minority in Ireland and Scotland until they turned the tables a few centuries ago.<br /> <br /> So I speculate that Blood-Blessed don't have any more to do with the Lancea et Sanctum than the Weihan Cynn. This has me wondering what Welsh and Cornish vampires followed, given that they would have started off as proto-Weihan Cynn*, but the mortals remained Christian on the whole since they were part of the Roman Empire. I imagine for them, it would be a bit more like the average werewolf or changeling's experience.<br /> <br /> *As I recall, it is implied that British vampires existed as some like the Weihan Cynn before the Romans came.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3523-spencer-from-the-hills","name":"Spencer from The Hills","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"546"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}